That concern makes sense. **The scientific boundary doesn’t need to be negotiable just because the conflict is mediated.** What can be discussed is how decisions are communicated, reviewed, and escalated—not whether the lab may knowingly present folklore as scientific fact.

You can agree only with explicit limits, preferably in writing before Friday:

> “I’m willing to participate in mediation concerning communication, working relationships, and our process for reviewing disputed outreach content. My participation should not be understood as agreement that unsupported claims may be presented as established science, or that scientific accuracy is subject to interpersonal compromise. I’d like the session to produce a clear, evidence-based publication and escalation policy.”

You can also ask for confirmation that:

- the mediator is neutral;
- the disputed wording and evidence will be part of the record;
- you won’t be required to publish, endorse, or apologize for rejecting misinformation;
- any apology is limited to conduct or tone, if warranted—not the accuracy decision;
- future disputes will follow a written review process, ideally involving the PI, communications lead, or subject-matter reviewer.

If they refuse those limits and insist that mediation means reconsidering whether misinformation can be published, you can decline **that framing** while remaining cooperative:

> “I’m open to resolving the interpersonal dispute, but I cannot negotiate the obligation to label folklore as folklore. Please clarify whether the proposed mediation concerns conduct and procedure or asks me to compromise scientific accuracy.”

One tactical point: instead of saying “I refuse mediation,” say **“I accept mediation under a clearly defined scope.”** That protects your boundary while making it harder to portray you as unwilling to collaborate. And if your manner was sharper than you intended, acknowledging that costs nothing scientifically: “I regret that the exchange became heated” is not the same as “I was wrong to stop the post.”

